<div class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">SUI</p>
  <h1>The UI framework agents write</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI assumes the author is a machine. Every decision a language leaves open
    is variance — a place where two agents diverge, or the same agent diverges
    from itself on Tuesday. So the decisions are gone. What's left is 95% of the
    expression code offers, with 10% of the choices.
  </p>

  <h2>What's given</h2>
  <p>
    Not recommended. Not linted with a warning. <em>Absent</em> — there is no
    second option to pick.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/elements">48 elements</a>, and you already know them.</strong>
      A dialog is <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>. A menu is <code>[popover]</code>.
      Half ship zero JavaScript; the rest compile away. A model that falls back
      on HTML lands on the standard library by accident.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/styling">One way to style.</a></strong> <code>sx</code>
      is a deliberate subset of CSS, keyed to the design system. An off-system
      color is a build error, not a review note.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/motion">One animation system.</a></strong>
      <code>motion:</code> — eight triggers, nine presets. You can't reach for a
      different library, so no two screens move differently.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/behavior">One way to reach an element.</a></strong>
      <code>use:</code>. No ref to thread, no imperative escape hatch to be
      tempted by.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you write</h2>
  <p>
    Composition and logic — the part that actually needs intelligence.
    <a href="/reactivity">Signals</a> for state,
    <a href="/control-flow">control flow</a> for shape,
    <a href="/data">live data</a> and forms with the plumbing already done.
  </p>

  <h2>And the compiler answers back</h2>
  <p>
    An agent writing React gets no oracle: a wrong program renders, plausibly,
    and ships. Here, a wrong program is <em>told what is wrong</em>, in the
    language's own vocabulary, the moment it is written. Agents run in a loop —
    a language that can correct them in one turn beats one they happen to have
    memorized.
  </p>

  <h2>None of which costs you performance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Parity with Solid across the js-framework-benchmark suite — faster on two of nine.</li>
    <li><strong>9.4&nbsp;kB</strong> gzipped. React is 59.8.</li>
    <li>Every element rendered in every state across all seven themes, on every commit.</li>
  </ul>

